    topics: [
      {
        id: 'http-methods', title: 'HTTP Methods',
        content: 'HTTP defines several request methods that indicate the desired action on a resource. GET retrieves data without side effects and is idempotent, meaning repeated calls produce the same result. POST creates new resources and is not idempotent — retrying may create duplicates. PUT replaces a resource entirely and is idempotent. PATCH partially updates a resource. DELETE removes a resource and is idempotent. HEAD returns only headers, useful for checking if a resource exists. OPTIONS returns allowed methods for a resource, critical for CORS preflight requests. In Angular, the HttpClient provides methods for each: this.http.get(), this.http.post(), this.http.put(), this.http.patch(), this.http.delete(). Understanding idempotency is crucial for designing reliable APIs — idempotent methods (GET, PUT, DELETE) are safe to retry on network failures.'
      },
      {
        id: 'status-codes', title: 'HTTP Status Codes',
        content: 'Status codes communicate the result of an HTTP request. The 1xx range indicates informational responses like 100 Continue. The 2xx range signals success: 200 OK is the standard success response, 201 Created is returned after successful resource creation via POST, and 204 No Content indicates success with no response body. The 3xx range handles redirection: 301 Moved Permanently tells clients to update bookmarks, 304 Not Modified enables conditional caching with ETags. The 4xx range represents client errors: 400 Bad Request for invalid syntax, 401 Unauthorized for authentication required, 403 Forbidden for authorization failures, 404 Not Found when resources do not exist, 409 Conflict for state conflicts, and 429 Too Many Requests for rate limiting. The 5xx range covers server errors: 500 Internal Server Error for unexpected failures and 503 Service Unavailable when servers are temporarily overloaded.'
      },
      {
        id: 'https-tls', title: 'HTTPS and TLS',
        content: 'HTTPS wraps HTTP in TLS (Transport Layer Security) encryption. The TLS handshake begins with a ClientHello message listing supported cipher suites. The server responds with its certificate and chosen cipher suite. The client verifies the certificate chain against trusted Certificate Authorities. Both parties perform key exchange to establish a shared secret. TLS 1.3, the latest version, reduces the handshake to a single round-trip (1-RTT) and supports 0-RTT resumption for returning connections. HSTS (HTTP Strict Transport Security) forces browsers to use HTTPS exclusively: Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains. Modern certificates from Let Encrypt are free and auto-renew via certbot. Always redirect HTTP to HTTPS and enable HSTS with a long max-age. Monitor certificate expiry to prevent outages.'
      },
      {
        id: 'http2-http3', title: 'HTTP/2 and HTTP/3',
        content: 'HTTP/2 solves HTTP/1.1 limitations with multiplexing — multiple requests and responses travel simultaneously over a single TCP connection, eliminating head-of-line blocking at the HTTP layer. It uses binary framing instead of text, HPACK header compression, and can push resources proactively via server push. HTTP/3 builds on QUIC instead of TCP, eliminating TCP-level head-of-line blocking entirely. QUIC combines transport and encryption in one protocol, enabling faster connection establishment with 0-RTT. HTTP/3 performs better on lossy networks since packet loss on one stream does not block others. To enable HTTP/2 in nginx, use listen 443 ssl http2. For HTTP/3, use listen 443 quic reuseport with the Alt-Svc header. All modern browsers support both protocols, making them essential for performance optimization.'
      }
    ]
