      {
        id: 'rest-principles', title: 'REST Principles',
        content: 'REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style with six constraints. Client-Server separates UI from data storage, enabling independent evolution. Statelessness means every request contains all necessary information — the server stores no client context. Cacheable responses must declare themselves cacheable or not. Uniform Interface defines four sub-constraints: resource identification via URIs, resource manipulation through representations, self-descriptive messages, and HATEOAS (Hypermedia as the Engine of Application State). Layered System allows clients to interact with intermediaries like load balancers or CDNs without knowing the direct server. Code on Demand is optional — servers can temporarily extend client functionality. Applying these constraints produces APIs that are scalable, maintainable, and evolvable. Resource-based design means URLs represent nouns (/users, /orders) and HTTP methods represent verbs (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE).'
      },
      {
        id: 'resource-naming', title: 'Resource Naming',
        content: 'REST resource naming follows predictable conventions. Use plural nouns for collections: /users for all users, /users/123 for user with ID 123. Express relationships through nesting: /users/123/orders for orders belonging to user 123, /products/456/reviews for reviews of product 456. Avoid verbs in URLs — instead of /getUsers or /createOrder, use GET /users and POST /orders. Use query parameters for filtering: /users?status=active, /products?category=electronics&sort=price. For actions that do not map cleanly to CRUD, use sub-resources: POST /users/123/activate instead of PUT /users/123 with an active flag. Search endpoints use a dedicated path: /users?q=john or /search/products?q=laptop. Keep URLs shallow — prefer /users/123 over /api/v1/organization/456/department/789/users/123. Case conventions typically use kebab-case for multi-word segments: /user-profiles, not /userProfiles.'
      },
      {
        id: 'pagination', title: 'Pagination Patterns',
        content: 'Pagination prevents returning entire datasets in a single response. Offset-based pagination uses offset and limit parameters: /users?offset=0&limit=20 for the first page, offset=20 for the second. It is simple but can skip or duplicate items when data changes between requests. Cursor-based pagination uses an opaque cursor returned in the response: /users?cursor=abc123&limit=20. The cursor encodes the position and is stable across data changes, making it reliable for real-time data. Page-based pagination serves UI pagination controls: /users?page=3&perPage=20 with total count in the response. Every paginated response should include metadata: total count, hasMore flag, nextCursor or next page number. Limit defaults to 20-50 items to balance completeness with performance. Always cap the maximum limit to prevent abuse — enforce a server-side maximum of 100 items per request.'
      },
      {
        id: 'versioning', title: 'API Versioning',
        content: 'API versioning manages breaking changes without disrupting existing clients. URL path versioning is most common and explicit: /api/v1/users, /api/v2/users. It is cacheable, discoverable, and simple to implement. Header versioning uses Accept or custom headers: Accept: application/vnd.myapp.v2+json. It keeps URLs clean but is less discoverable. Query parameter versioning adds ?version=2 — simple but not cacheable. For deprecation, provide 6-12 months notice, add Sunset and Deprecation headers, return 410 Gone for removed endpoints, and publish migration guides. In Angular, create an HttpInterceptor that prefixes URLs with the current API version. Consider content negotiation over versioning when possible — new fields in responses are backward-compatible and do not require version bumps. Only create new versions when you must change or remove existing fields or break response structures.'
      }
