        content: 'Cache-Control directives control caching behavior. public allows any cache (CDN, browser) to store the response. private restricts caching to the browser only. no-cache means the response can be cached but must be revalidated before each use. no-store prevents any caching. max-age specifies freshness duration in seconds. s-maxage sets a separate max-age for shared caches (CDNs). immutable indicates the content will never change — ideal for hashed filenames. must-revalidate forces revalidation when stale. For hashed static assets (JS, CSS with content hash), use Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable. For HTML pages that change frequently, use no-cache to always check for updates. For API data, use private, max-age=60, must-revalidate. For sensitive data, use no-store. The cache hierarchy flows from browser (private) to CDN edge (s-maxage) to origin server — each level respects its own directives.'
