        id: 'etag-validation', title: 'ETag and Conditional Requests',
        content: 'ETags enable conditional requests that save bandwidth. When a server returns a response, it includes an ETag header with a unique identifier for that version. When the cached response becomes stale, the browser sends an If-None-Match header with the ETag value. If the resource has not changed, the server returns 304 Not Modified with no body — the browser uses the cached version. If the ETag does not match, the server returns 200 OK with the new content and updated ETag. Strong ETags (no prefix) guarantee byte-level equivalence — any change in the response body produces a different ETag. Weak ETags (W/ prefix) indicate semantic equivalence — formatting changes do not alter the ETag. ETags work with Last-Modified as a fallback validation mechanism. In Angular, implement client-side caching by checking ETag headers before making requests. Combine ETags with Cache-Control max-age for optimal freshness and bandwidth savings.'
