        content: 'Scaling WebSockets beyond a single server requires message routing across instances. Sticky sessions (source IP hashing) route all connections from a client to the same server — simple but prevents true horizontal scaling and causes uneven load. A message broker (Redis Pub/Sub, NATS, Kafka) decouples message routing from connection hosting. Each server instance subscribes to relevant channels; when a message arrives, it is published to the broker, which fans it out to all instances with subscribed clients. Redis Pub/Sub is the simplest approach — use channels per room or per user. For high-throughput scenarios, NATS or Kafka provide durable message delivery and replay. Connection affinity with a consistent hashing ring maps users to specific servers, reducing cross-instance communication. Server discovery lets clients find the optimal server — DNS SRV records or a service registry provide server lists. Health checks detect failed instances and redistribute connections. Memory management limits per-connection buffer sizes and total server memory. Monitor active connections, message throughput, and latency per instance. Deploy WebSocket servers behind a load balancer that supports WebSocket upgrade — nginx, HAProxy, or AWS ALB. Consider serverless WebSockets (AWS API Gateway WebSockets) for automatic scaling without managing infrastructure.'
